.page netstat 1 show network connections, routes and interface statistics

# SYNOPSIS
*netstat* [*-altun*]
*netstat* *-r*
*netstat* *-i*

# DESCRIPTION
`netstat` reads `/proc/net` and prints what the network stack is doing. Without options it lists the TCP and UDP sockets that are connected; `-l` lists the ones that are waiting for someone (listening TCP sockets and bound, unconnected UDP sockets), `-a` lists both kinds.

The columns are the protocol, the bytes waiting to be read (`Recv-Q`) and not yet acknowledged by the other side (`Send-Q`), the local and foreign address with port (`*` is any address), and the TCP state: `ESTABLISHED`, `SYN_SENT`, `SYN_RECV`, `FIN_WAIT1`, `FIN_WAIT2`, `TIME_WAIT`, `CLOSE_WAIT`, `LAST_ACK`, `LISTEN`, `CLOSING`. A closed connection stays in `TIME_WAIT` for half a minute so that stray packets of the old conversation cannot confuse a new one.

# OPTIONS
@ *-a*
    All sockets, listening or not.
@ *-l*
    Only listening sockets.
@ *-t*, *-u*
    Only TCP, only UDP.
@ *-n*
    Accepted for compatibility; addresses are always numeric.
@ *-r*
    The routing table, like route -n.
@ *-i*
    One line of packet counters per interface.

# FILES
@ /proc/net/tcp, /proc/net/udp, /proc/net/dev, /proc/net/route
    Where the numbers come from.

# SEE ALSO
ss(1), route(1), ifconfig(1), network(7)
